# TODO - Adaptive semantic profile width

## Objective

Determine the smallest semantic ternary width that is provably sufficient for
one compiled program, initially within `10<=N<=14`, and execute only that
`3^N`-word memory geometry when equivalence is established. Treat width as a
verified program property rather than always paying the canonical 14-trit
resident-memory cost. Feed the proved width into resource planning so small
programs can admit more simultaneous accelerator work while larger GPUs use
all measured resources instead of stopping at a device-specific artificial
ceiling.

If compiler and verifier evidence proves that a program's complete reachable
behavior is invariant under replacing its declared profile by a smaller width
`N`, then its semantic memory image has exactly `3^N` words instead of `3^14`.
The memory-word-count ratios are therefore 81x, 27x, 9x, 3x, and 1x for widths
10 through 14. Total resident bytes need not fall by the same factor because
registers, buffers, metadata, transfers, verification, and other resources add
separate costs; resource evidence must measure those costs directly.

## Acceptance

- A width proof covers every semantics affected by `N`: address reachability,
  pointer wraparound, memory initialization and recurrence, word modulus,
  rotate/crazy behavior, self-modification, I/O-visible values, and termination.
- Program size alone is never a proof of a smaller width. Adversarial fixtures
  include small artifacts whose later recurrence, wrapping, or pointers require
  a larger semantic geometry.
- The trusted verifier independently checks the selected width; optimizer,
  compiler heuristics, CUDA, and resource planners cannot declare equivalence.
- Width selection is deterministic and fail-closed. If no smaller width is
  proved, execution retains the canonical required profile unchanged.
- Resident memory accounting uses the selected semantic `3^N` geometry rather
  than a padded arithmetic width. The `5+5+5` arithmetic-padding experiment is
  owned separately by `symmetric-padded-crazy-chunk-geometry`.
- Accelerator planning discovers actual device memory and compute resources at
  runtime. A smaller device may admit less work, while an RTX 5090-class or any
  future larger device may consume additional measured capacity without a
  hard-coded upper VRAM or batch ceiling.
- Resource evidence reports exact bytes per resident VM, admitted concurrent
  states or chunks, launch geometry, failure/fallback behavior, and end-to-end
  throughput. Synthetic large-memory probes are labelled capacity evidence,
  not hardware-speed measurements.
- Benchmarks cover widths 10 through 14 under equivalent work and include both
  memory-bound and compute/search-heavy cases. Retained null or negative
  results constrain selection rather than being discarded.
- No claim that compilation time stays bounded as program length grows follows
  from narrower state alone. Search/pruning scaling requires separate admissible
  mathematics and retained scaling evidence.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Required mathematical evidence: exact profile-width memory ratios and a
  sufficient-condition theorem or certificate format for semantic narrowing.
- The mathematical prerequisite is now satisfied by durable correspondences;
  the broader `malbolge-specific-optimization-mathematics` TODO remains active
  independently and no longer blocks this task.
- Current mathematical base: correspondences
  `optimization-profile-width-projection-audit`,
  `optimization-profile-width-source-admission-monotonicity`,
  `optimization-profile-width-initial-memory-projection`,
  `optimization-profile-width-decode-compatible-count`,
  `optimization-profile-width-encryption-invariance`,
  `optimization-profile-width-input-projection`,
  `optimization-profile-width-graphical-fetch-mismatch-count`,
  `optimization-profile-width-finite-bisimulation-certificate`, and
  `optimization-profile-width-minimum-certified-selector` establish checked
  projection/loader laws, a fail-closed finite certificate shape, and the
  deterministic minimum-width choice. No compiled program has yet been
  certified narrower.
- Required correctness evidence: differential/reference execution plus
  adversarial width-boundary tests and independent verification of certificates.
- First projection-preserving positive fixture: source `QP` decodes to halt at
  position zero and preserves complete current-14 to historical-10 state
  projection before and after termination for both available-byte and EOF input.
  Because halt performs no I/O, encryption, or pointer advance, this fixture is
  input-independent within the tested byte-stream interface. It remains
  research evidence rather than a product-issued certificate.
- Research certificate schema v1 remains parseable as structural relation
  evidence. Schema v2 adds an explicit proof kind plus exact source bytes and
  exact `input_id -> byte stream` bindings. Positive selection accepts only v2
  certificates whose binding matches the externally requested source/input
  subject byte-for-byte; v1 has no positive selection authority. The retained
  `qp-width-certificate-v2.json` binds source `QP`, byte input `0xA5`, EOF, and
  proof kind `initial-halt-projection-v1`.
- Certificate parsing rejects unknown fields, ambiguous scalar types, malformed
  state surfaces, binding drift, or unknown proof kinds. Neither schema grants
  trusted runtime-selection authority.
- The research selector consumes the externally requested source/input subject
  plus exact decisions for all candidate widths 10 through 13. `False` is an
  explicit rejection; positive selection requires a bound v2 certificate whose
  source/input bytes match that subject, whose narrow width matches the
  candidate, whose wide width is canonical 14, and whose recognized proof kind
  validates. Bare `True`, legacy v1, missing decisions, subject drift, width
  mismatches, or malformed certificates fail closed to 14.
- A theorem-specific research checker certifies the initial-halt family without
  trusting final differential equality. It checks widths, source capacity and
  lexical admission, every position-dependent loader decode, and an initial `v`
  halt before any width-sensitive transition effect. Its exact admission
  decisions are cross-checked against the independent verifier decode model over
  all 94 phases and all 94 graphical cells. The `QP` certificate keeps byte and
  EOF state lineages distinct because remaining input is part of complete state.
- Proof kind `noop-prefix-halt-projection-v1` extends that base to any nonempty
  prefix of decoded `o` instructions followed by `v`. Each no-op leaves A and
  I/O unchanged, applies the same graphical self-encryption at the same low
  code address, and advances C/D together; the next source cell therefore stays
  in the proved projection relation. `DP` is the retained two-step certificate,
  while `DCBA@?>=I` checks complete 14-to-10 projection after eight consecutive
  no-ops and the following halt for byte and EOF inputs.
- Proof kind `input-then-halt-projection-v1` covers decoded `/` followed by `v`.
  Available bytes are identical across widths, while each wide EOF word projects
  exactly to the corresponding narrow EOF word; self-encryption and pointer
  advance then preserve the relation until halt. Fixture `uP` retains both a
  consumed-byte lineage and an EOF lineage with their different
  `input_consumed` observations.
- Proof kind `input-output-halt-projection-v1` covers decoded `/`, `<`, `v`
  only when every exact declared input stream is nonempty. Then input stores the
  same byte at both widths and output emits that same byte; an EOF-capable
  domain is rejected because the projected EOF words have different residues
  modulo 256. Fixture `ubO` certifies the bound `0xA5` domain, while a Rust
  adversarial run shows projection surviving input and breaking exactly at
  output under EOF.
- Proof kind `straight-line-safe-projection-v1` composes admitted straight-line
  prefixes over `o`, `/`, `<`, `j`, and guarded `p`, followed by `v`. For each
  exact input stream it tracks input cursor and whether A is byte-exact or
  merely
  projection-related; `<` is accepted only in the byte-exact state. Independent
  bounded enumeration covers every length-one-through-four prefix over `o`, `/`,
  `<`, and at most one `j`, with streams of lengths zero through two.
- D is tracked separately as an exact physical address or as projection-only.
  A `j` at exact D preserves the step; `initial_memory_word` compares concrete
  N/14 words to decide whether resulting D remains exact. Thus `jjv` may halt
  with projected D, while `jjjv` remains exact through recurrence cells 41 and
  43. Width-10 initialization is cross-checked against the independent verifier.
- Fixture `uCar_L` (`/ o < / < v`) selects width 10 for two exact bytes, while a
  one-byte subject diverges exactly at the second output. In `j j p o v`, crazy
  still produces projected accumulators, yet D names different physical cells,
  so the memory-write relation breaks; memory operations therefore require exact
  D rather than merely projected D.
- Guarded `p` is admitted only after that exact-address `j`, with D distinct
  from C, in-domain, and not targeting an unexecuted source cell. `p` marks A as
  projection-only, so later output fails closed until byte input restores exact
  A. Endpoint runs preserve complete projection for `j p v` and `j p p v`;
  unguarded `p v` rejects its non-graphical encryption target in both VMs.
- The same bound `uCar_L` certificate is checked independently at narrow widths
  10, 11, 12, and 13. Selector evidence accepts each candidate when it is the
  sole positive certificate, chooses that exact width, and returns canonical 14
  when all four candidates are explicit rejections; no monotonic inference is
  used between widths.
- Widths 11 through 13 are derived execution geometries, not new canonical
  `ProfileDescriptor` identities. Research geometry output carries only
  `word_trits=N` and exactly `3^N` memory words: 59,049; 177,147; 531,441;
  1,594,323; and 4,782,969 for N=10 through 14. Invalid selection evidence maps
  to the canonical 14-trit geometry. Runtime integration must preserve the
  canonical language/profile identity while consuming this derived geometry.
- Synthetic resource-budget evidence now feeds those five derived geometries
  into the product resident planner without changing profile identity. For a
  zero-I/O resident item, exact backend layout is 16 state words plus `3^N`
  memory words at four bytes each, with eight fixed bytes per chunk. Item sizes
  are 236,260; 708,652; 2,125,828; 6,377,356; and 19,131,940 bytes for N=10
  through 14. Under the same synthetic 128 MiB resource snapshot, first-chunk
  capacities are 532, 177, 59, 19, and 6 complete VMs respectively.
- This remains capacity evidence only; it does not grant runtime width-selection
  authority or satisfy the still-required equivalent-work throughput
